      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="industry-applications">
        <h2>Industry-Specific Application Guide for Steel Substrate Chemistry</h2>
        <p>
          The relative importance and specification stringency of each steel substrate chemistry discipline
          shifts significantly by industry. Offshore oil and gas imposes the most rigorous coating
          specification requirements — ISO 12944 C5-M or CX, NORSOK M-501, and SSPC Paint 20 Level 3
          zinc-rich primers are standard — while adhesive bonding and lubricant selection follow
          client-specific or API standards for rotating equipment and piping. Automotive OEM manufacturing
          runs all four disciplines simultaneously on every vehicle body: alkaline spray wash and zinc
          phosphating in the pre-treatment tunnel, cathodic electrocoat primer, structural adhesives
          in body-in-white welding jigs, and metalworking fluids on all machined components, with
          rust preventive applied to all machined surfaces before final assembly. Infrastructure and
          construction projects specify to ISO 12944 or national equivalents for structural steelwork,
          with anaerobic threadlockers and thread sealants on bolted connections and standard NLGI 2
          grease on bearings and slideways.
        </p>
        <p>
          Heavy industry — steel mills, mining, cement, and paper — places the greatest demands on
          lubricant performance: shock-loaded open gears require asphaltic or synthetic open gear compounds;
          high-temperature furnace areas require heat-stable synthetic ester or silicone-base greases;
          wet and contaminated environments require EP greases with superior water-washout resistance
          (typically lithium complex or calcium sulphonate complex thickener systems). In these environments,
          the cleaning discipline is less about pre-coating surface preparation and more about
          continuous maintenance degreasing — keeping machinery clean enough for inspection, lubrication
          access, and condition monitoring. Solvent-based industrial degreasers remain the most
          effective option for heavy oil and bitumen removal in these environments, though REACH
          restrictions on chlorinated solvents have shifted much of this market toward high-boiling
          hydrocarbon blends and bio-based degreasers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 7 -->
      <section id="cost-practical">
        <h2>Cost, Availability, and Practical Considerations</h2>
        <p>
          The cost structure of steel substrate chemistry is dominated not by the cost of the chemical
          product itself but by the cost of surface preparation, application labour, and the consequences
          of failure. A high-specification ISO 12944 C5-M coating system applied to blast-cleaned steel
          may cost several times more per square metre than a basic alkyd paint, but the C5-M system
          provides 25+ year durability versus 5–7 years for the alkyd — with every repainting cycle
          requiring scaffold erection, blast cleaning, and production downtime that typically exceeds
          the material cost by a large margin. The economic case for specifying to the correct durability
          class is straightforward: the highest-performance coating system is almost always the most
          economical over the asset life. The same principle applies to lubricant specification: an
          API GL-5 gear oil costs more than GL-4, but one catastrophic gear failure — replacement of
          a heavy industrial gearbox plus production downtime — costs multiples of the annual lubricant
          consumption for the entire facility.
        </p>
        <p>
          Regulatory compliance adds an increasingly significant practical dimension. EU REACH restrictions
          affect all four steel substrate chemistry disciplines: VOC limits restrict solvent content in
          coatings (Decopaint Directive); isocyanate regulations impose training obligations on 1K PU
          adhesive and sealant users; chlorinated EP additive restrictions eliminate SCCPs from gear
          oil and metalworking fluid formulations; and chlorinated solvent restrictions reduce cleaning
          options in maintenance degreasing. Products sold into EU, UK, or other REACH-aligned markets
          must be formulated around these restrictions — SVHC-containing chemistries that remain
          available for export markets are not appropriate for European supply chains. Specifiers and
          formulators should verify compliance status of all chemical products against current ECHA
          candidate list, Authorisation List (Annex XIV), and Restriction List (Annex XVII) before
          finalising specifications.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What surface preparation is required before applying a coating to steel?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The surface preparation standard determines the adhesion, performance, and service life of any coating on steel more than any other single factor. ISO 8501-1 defines blast-cleaning grades Sa 1 (light blast), Sa 2 (thorough blast), Sa 2½ (very thorough blast — the most commonly specified for industrial protective coatings), and Sa 3 (blast to white metal). For high-performance protective coatings in C4 or C5 corrosivity environments per ISO 12944, Sa 2½ minimum is required. Power tool cleaning to St 2 or St 3 may be acceptable for lower-corrosivity environments or maintenance recoating over existing intact paint. All hydrocarbon contamination must be removed by solvent washing or alkaline degreasing before blasting — blast cleaning over oiled steel embeds contaminants into the surface profile rather than removing them.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Which structural adhesive works best for bonding steel?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The best structural adhesive for steel bonding depends on the required joint performance: load type, operating temperature, environmental exposure, and assembly process. Two-component epoxy adhesives provide the highest shear and tensile bond strengths on properly prepared steel and are preferred for high-load static structural applications. Two-component acrylic (MMA) adhesives tolerate less surface preparation, provide faster fixture times, and offer better impact resistance — widely used in industrial fabrication and automotive structural applications. One-component polyurethane adhesives offer flexibility and vibration resistance for joints that must absorb dynamic loads or differential thermal movement. Anaerobic adhesives cure in the metal-activated, oxygen-free annular gap and are used for thread locking and cylindrical assembly. For all systems, solvent cleaning and light abrasion of the steel surface improve bond strength measurably.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can you coat steel without blast cleaning if a grinder is not available?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes, but with significant limitations on coating system performance and service life. Hand and power tool cleaning to ISO 8501-1 St 2 or St 3 can remove loose rust, scale, and paint but cannot create the surface profile that grit blasting achieves. Without a surface profile, most high-build epoxy primers do not achieve their rated adhesion values. For steel in low-to-moderate corrosivity environments (ISO 12944 C1–C3) or for maintenance recoating over intact existing paint, St 3 power tool cleaning combined with a surface-tolerant primer can provide acceptable performance. Surface-tolerant epoxy primers formulated with penetrating vehicles bond to marginally prepared steel and are a practical solution for maintenance painting where blasting is impractical — but are not a substitute for proper blast cleaning in high-corrosivity or immersed service.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between alkaline and acid cleaners for steel?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Alkaline and acid cleaners address different contamination types and must not be used interchangeably. Alkaline degreasers (pH 9–14, based on sodium hydroxide, silicates, and surfactants) remove organic contamination — mineral oils, cutting fluids, and grease — through saponification and emulsification. They do not dissolve rust or mill scale. Acid cleaners (hydrochloric, phosphoric, citric acid) remove inorganic contamination — rust and mill scale — through dissolution. Phosphoric acid removes rust and simultaneously deposits an iron phosphate conversion coating that improves primer adhesion. The correct sequence for heavily contaminated steel is alkaline degreasing first, rinse, then acid treatment if required, rinse again, then coat while still fresh — never reverse this order.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What lubricant is required for steel gears operating under high contact loads?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Steel gears operating under high contact loads — particularly hypoid and spiral bevel gear pairs with high sliding velocities — require gear oils containing extreme pressure (EP) additive packages. API GL-5 is the minimum requirement for hypoid rear axle gears, using sulphur-phosphorus EP chemistry that activates at asperity contact temperatures to deposit sacrificial iron sulphide films preventing welding and scoring. Industrial spur and helical gear reducers in ISO VG 220 to 460 grades typically require gear oils passing FZG stage 12 or higher under ISO 14635-1. The EP additive chemistry must be matched to the metal type: aggressive EP packages suited to steel-on-steel can corrode yellow metal synchroniser and thrust-washer components, so always confirm compatibility with non-ferrous components before specifying.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does ISO 12944 define corrosivity categories for steel coating selection?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            ISO 12944-2 defines six atmospheric corrosivity categories (C1 through C5, plus CX for extreme environments) based on annual mass loss of standard carbon steel and zinc reference specimens. C1 covers dry indoor environments; C2 unheated structures with occasional condensation; C3 urban and industrial atmospheres; C4 industrial and coastal environments with moderate salinity; C5 offshore, chemical plant, and permanent condensation environments; CX covers offshore structures and strong chemical exposure. Each category determines the minimum coating system design — DFT per coat, number of coats, and primer type — and the expected durability period. Specifying to the correct ISO 12944 corrosivity category and durability class is the most important single decision in protective coating system design for steel.
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>

